Economic Viability and Supply-Chain Disconnection Resilience

A Data-Feasibility, Multiverse, and Retrodiction Framework for Productive Systems with Computational Primitives


Ouadi Maakoul + SI TEAM


Abstract

Terrestrial viability metrics presuppose a recovery assumption: that disrupted supply can eventually be re-established. Removing that assumption does not merely add noise to viability as a predictor of resilience; it changes the object being measured. This thesis asks under what conditions the removal of the recovery assumption causes terrestrial viability to diverge from, or invert against, the ability of a productive system to maintain functional output, and it specifies the computational primitives required to answer the question.

The framework has six components, each with an executable specification:

· A conditional-divergence formulation \Delta V(P \mid f) with z-scored viability as the primary normalization, addressing the magnitude loss of rank-based normalization.
· A dependency-graph formalism with a productive-closure operator \Phi_R, reported in three currencies (functional, mass-weighted, information), with a borrowed-closure flag \beta(a) and closure-chain depth \delta_R(a).
· A reconfiguration-resilience decomposition estimated through variance decomposition, separating repair from closure expansion.
· A preregistered multiverse with Post-Selection Inference in Multiverse Analysis (PIMA) as the inferential engine, resolving the descriptive-only limitation of multiverse analysis and the linear-model restriction of specification-curve inference.
· A retrodiction discriminability gate with era-fixed inventory policy against two documented disruptions (Tōhoku 2011, automotive semiconductor shortage 2021).
· A reflexive anchoring standard that labels every parameter and every decision threshold as either anchored (externally supported) or conventional (researcher-specified), applied to the thesis's own NO-GO rules.

Every result—aligned, independent, conflicting, or NO-GO—is informative. The computational primitives are specified as typed data structures and monotone operators, permitting direct implementation and Lean 4 export.

---

1. Introduction and motivation

1.1 Problem. A productive system can be profitable because it is deeply embedded in global trade; that same embedding can make it fragile. Terrestrial viability metrics presuppose that disrupted inputs can eventually be re-sourced. This is the recovery assumption. The literature distinguishes resilience (recovery to a referenced normal state) from viability (adaptation without a referenced normal state; structural reconfiguration toward a new equilibrium) precisely on this ground. A multiplanetary economy cannot make the recovery assumption.

1.2 The reframed gap. Existing work treats the two sides separately: industrial economics optimizes value under an implicit recovery assumption; resilience engineering measures recovery assuming recovery is possible; space-infrastructure studies analyze self-sufficiency with mass-based closure metrics. The sharper gap is that terrestrial viability is measured with an assumption that is definitionally unavailable in the target context, and no framework exists for estimating when removing that assumption causes viability to diverge from—or invert against—the resilience criterion.

1.3 Contributions.

1. A conditional-divergence formulation \Delta V(P \mid f) with z-score primary normalization.
2. A productive-closure operator reported in three currencies (\kappa_{\text{func}}, \kappa_{\text{mass}}, \kappa_{\text{info}}) with a borrowed-closure flag and chain depth.
3. A reconfiguration-resilience decomposition estimated through variance decomposition.
4. A preregistered multiverse with PIMA as the inferential layer, providing family-wise error control across specifications.
5. A retrodiction gate with era-fixed inventory policy.
6. A reflexive anchoring standard applied to the thesis's own thresholds.

1.4 Scope exclusions. Vacuum, dust, radiation, and low-gravity engineering are out of scope. Governance is deferred and treated only as a conceptual analogy. Build order is deferred to a second paper because it is path-dependent.

---

2. Research questions

Primary. Under what conditions does the removal of the recovery assumption—formalized as progressive reduction of external supply—cause terrestrial economic viability to diverge from, or invert against, the ability of a productive system to maintain functional output?

Secondary.

· What is the conditional-divergence function \Delta V(P \mid f), and does it exhibit a pre-registered threshold f^* beyond which divergence becomes practically meaningful?
· Which dependency indicators—including closure-chain depth \delta_R and borrowed-closure status—predict function-level failure, out of sample, relative to the baselines in H2?
· Does the Pareto frontier between viability and structural resilience exhibit an identifiable knee?
· Do public data support these questions at all, as tested by the retrodiction gate and the aggregation-bias test?

