3. Theoretical framework and computational primitives

3.1 Productive system and provenance schema

A productive system is a tuple \mathbf{S} = (G, I, K, E, L, \Pi, R). Every node and recipe is accompanied by a provenance record that enforces the anchoring standard at the level of data entry.

Definition 3.1 (Provenance record). A provenance record is a tuple

\pi = (\text{id}, \text{source\_type}, \text{url}, \text{location}, \text{TRL}, c, \ell)

where \text{source\_type} \in \{\text{BOM}, \text{CAD}, \text{datasheet}, \text{expert}\}, \text{TRL} \in \{1,\dots,9\}, c \in [0,1] is a confidence weight, and

\ell \in \{\text{anchored}, \text{unanchored-structural}, \text{unanchored-operational}, \text{conventional}\}

is the anchoring label. A resource node v carries mass m(v), an information-complexity weight w_{\text{info}}(v), a criticality flag, and a lead time \tau_L(v). A recipe \rho is a tuple

\rho = (\text{id}, \text{output}, \text{inputs}, \text{tools}, \text{energy}, \text{variant}, \pi)

with \text{inputs} an AND-set, \text{tools} \subseteq K, and \text{variant} \in \{\text{R\_strict}, \text{R\_moderate}, \text{R\_permissive}\}.

3.2 Dependency graph and structural indicators

Let G = (V,A) be a directed AND/OR graph. Arcs u \to v denote requirement. AND-nodes require all inputs; OR-nodes require any one, capturing documented substitutes. Structural indicators: single points of failure; dependencies without substitutes; independent alternative paths; cascade size under single-node removal; lead-time-weighted exposure; closure-chain depth \delta_R (§3.3); borrowed-closure fraction \bar{\beta} (§3.3). Criticality rules are fixed before any simulation.

3.3 Productive closure: three currencies, chain depth, borrowed closure

Let M be available materials, E available energy, K installed equipment, R the recipe set. Define the monotone operator

\Phi_R(X) = X \cup \left\{ a : \exists \rho \in R \text{ for } a \text{ with inputs}(\rho) \subseteq X,\ \text{tools}(\rho) \subseteq K,\ \text{energy}(\rho) \text{ feasible under } E \right\}.

\Phi_R is monotone on the finite lattice (2^V, \subseteq), so by the Knaster–Tarski theorem a least fixed point exists:

\mathcal{P}_R(M,E,K) = \operatorname{lfp}(\Phi_R).

A part is locally reproducible only if it lies in \mathcal{P}_R. The fixed-point construction follows recursive dependency to its limit.

Three closure currencies. With C_{\text{crit}} the pre-registered critical set and m(a) the mass of a:

\kappa_{\text{func}} = \frac{|\mathcal{P}_R \cap C_{\text{crit}}|}{|C_{\text{crit}}|},
\qquad
\kappa_{\text{mass}} = \frac{\sum_{a \in \mathcal{P}_R \cap C_{\text{crit}}} m(a)}{\sum_{a \in C_{\text{crit}}} m(a)},

\kappa_{\text{info}} = \frac{\sum_{a \in \mathcal{P}_R \cap C_{\text{crit}}} w_{\text{info}}(a)}{\sum_{a \in C_{\text{crit}}} w_{\text{info}}(a)}.

\kappa_{\text{info}} is a novel operationalization: information closure is an established dimension of closure theory, but no standardized scalar metric exists. It is presented as a pre-registered construction, not a validated import. The mass-based closure ratio in the space-systems literature settles around 90–96% closure in optimistic designs, with the shortfall concentrated in "vitamin" parts—microelectronics, precision metrology, enriched materials—that constitute a small mass fraction but break full closure. Divergence among \kappa_{\text{func}}, \kappa_{\text{mass}}, and \kappa_{\text{info}} is itself informative.

Closure-chain depth.

\delta_R(a) = \text{longest chain length from } a \text{ to an external input along the AND/OR witness of } a \in \mathcal{P}_R.

Report mean, 95th percentile, fraction with \delta_R > 4, maximum, and the count of critical components whose witnessing chains converge on the same external node. Shallow \delta_R is structurally more resilient than membership via a long, fragile recursive chain.

Borrowed closure. For each a \in C_{\text{crit}},

\beta(a) = \mathbb{1}\left[\text{tools}(\rho_a) \not\subseteq \mathcal{P}_{R_{\text{strict}}}\right],
\qquad
\bar{\beta} = \frac{|\{a : \beta(a)=1\}|}{|C_{\text{crit}}|}.

A part with \beta(a)=1 is topologically closed but resourced from a host—the vitamin architecture.

Recipe-set sensitivity. Closure is computed under \text{R\_strict} (fully documented recipes), \text{R\_moderate} (documented plus explicitly labeled plausible substitutes), and \text{R\_permissive} (speculative substitutes, each requiring at least one external reference). If

\kappa_{\text{func}}(\text{permissive}) - \kappa_{\text{func}}(\text{strict}) > \theta_\kappa,

the conclusion is labeled recipe-conditional in the first sentence of the relevant section. \theta_\kappa is a pre-registered convention (§5.6).

3.4 Reconfiguration resilience

The "adaptive" component conflated repair (restoring designed function) with closure expansion (bringing a previously external input into \mathcal{P}_R). Draft 0.4 separates them and estimates the decomposition through variance decomposition.

Component Meaning Operational definition
Inventory Survives on stocks Output retained with repair and closure expansion disabled
Structural Survives by design Output retained at standardized inventory with reconfiguration disabled, explained by \delta_R and \bar{\beta}
Reconfiguration Survives by repair or closure expansion Gain in output when reconfiguration enabled vs disabled, split into repair and closure-expansion subcomponents

Variance decomposition. For configuration c and disruption d, with Y_{c,d} = P(t,S_d):

Y_{c,d} = \mu + \beta_I I_c + \boldsymbol{\beta}_G \mathbf{g}_c + \beta_R R_c + \beta_X (I_c \times R_c) + \boldsymbol{\beta}_Y (\mathbf{g}_c \times R_c) + u_c + \varepsilon_{c,d},

with \mathbf{g}_c the graph-indicator vector and u_c \sim \mathcal{N}(0,\sigma_u^2) a configuration random effect. Variance shares are estimated by nested-model comparison; interaction terms are estimated and reported before any main-component interpretation.

3.5 Economic value and conditional divergence

V_{\text{net}} = R - C_{\text{op}} - C_{\text{maint}} - C_{\text{repl}} - C_{\text{risk}}.

V_{\text{net}} is evaluated under five price regimes: P1 terrestrial prices, P2 high transport cost, P3 energy-constrained, P4a/P4b mass-constrained shadow prices from the dual of a resource-allocation program under two objectives (maximize aggregate output vs maximize \kappa_{\text{func}}) with identical constraints, and P5 resilience-adjusted shadow prices maximizing \lambda \cdot \text{Output} + (1-\lambda) \cdot \kappa_{\text{func}} with \lambda pre-registered.

Conditional divergence. For configuration c and supply-removal fraction f:

\Delta V(P \mid f) = \mathbb{E}[P(t,S) \mid f] - g(V_{\text{net}}(c) \mid f).

Normalization. Within multiverse cell m:

· Primary: g_{\text{primary}}(V) = (V - \mu_{V,m}) / \sigma_{V,m}, the z-score.
· Robustness: g_{\text{robust}}(V) = rank percentile within the cell.

The z-score is primary because rank normalization discards magnitude: configurations with symmetric rank movements can produce \Delta V \approx 0 and misclassify a conflicting case as independent.

Divergence threshold. f^* is the smallest f at which the bootstrap lower bound of |\Delta V| exceeds a pre-registered \delta_{\min} and the sign of \Delta V is stable for all f' > f on the pre-registered grid. \delta_{\min} is calibrated in 01C on the retrodiction data and frozen before 01D.

3.6 Disruption model and outcomes

A disruption is S = (f,d,k). Aggregate output ratio P_{\text{agg}}(t,S) = Q(t,S)/Q(0). With F_{\text{crit}} the critical-function set and thresholds \theta_f, function f fails at t if Q_f(t,S) < \theta_f.

Primary outcome: RMTL under competing risks. With M failure modes and cumulative incidence F_m(t):

\text{RMTL}_m(\tau) = \int_0^\tau F_m(t)\, dt.

RMTL is designed for competing-risks settings and measures event-related time loss per mode; simulation evidence shows transformed RMTL global tests control type I error across small samples and high censoring rates while improving power. The logit transformation is used when the event of interest is more frequent than competing events; the complementary log-log when competing events dominate. \tau = 2 \times median critical inventory depletion time is a pre-registered convention.

Secondary outcomes. \text{RMST}(\tau); area under P_{\text{agg}}; recovery time; cumulative unmet demand \int (D(t) - Q(t))_+ dt; function-level failure time.

Censoring diagnostics. Simulation censoring is informative. Report the censoring fraction per configuration and fit a logistic mixed model of censoring on graph indicators. If censoring is strongly associated with structural resilience, the RMST comparison is biased, and the bias direction is reported.

Disruptions are sampled from (f,d,k) with a Latin hypercube design. The design provides stratified coverage of the parameter space, preventing clustering and ensuring that edge cases are proportionally represented across configurations.

