5.2 Stage 01A: audit with operationalized NO-GO and aggregation-bias test

Structured minimum-data specification.

Domain Minimum variable set Consequence if below
Dependencies Bills of materials to component level NO-GO
Time Procurement and replacement lead times CONDITIONAL GO (lead-time claims excluded)
Substitution Documented alternatives with specification CONDITIONAL GO (substitution claims excluded)
Production Real transformation and fabrication capabilities CONDITIONAL GO (closure limited to \text{R\_strict})
Economics Prices, costs, alternative parameters CONDITIONAL GO (P1–P3 only)

Retrodiction discriminability gate with era-fixed policy. Two documented targets are pre-specified:

Target 1 — Tōhoku 2011, Renesas Naka MCU outage. Toyota's recovery to pre-quake production took approximately six months after the Naka plant was knocked out. The indirect economic damage is estimated at 6.9 trillion yen, approximately 40% of the direct impact, implying a propagation ratio of roughly 0.40 under the documented loss definition. Target 1 is evaluated under 2011 inventory policy: pre-earthquake just-in-time buffers with minimal redundant MCU inventory.

Target 2 — 2021 automotive semiconductor shortage. GM cut approximately 278,000 vehicles against an industry-wide loss of roughly 6.2 million units. Target 2 is evaluated under 2021 non-Toyota-buffer policy: lean just-in-time supply lines without the post-2011 business continuity inventory that Toyota had built.

Using 2021 Toyota inventory to retrodict the 2011 shock, or 2011 inventory to retrodict the 2021 shock, fails for a policy reason rather than a model reason. Era-fixed assignment is a necessary condition for gate validity.

\tau_{\text{retro}} is an interval on the propagation ratio, calibrated in 01C against the adopted loss definition, not imported as a fixed range.

Aggregation-bias test. Construct the graph at the finest available resolution and at an aggregated resolution. Establishment- and product-level evidence shows that industry-level aggregation understates propagation. If aggregated and finest responses differ substantively, the result is resolution-conditional. The difference thresholds (\Delta_{\text{agg}}^{\text{low}}, \Delta_{\text{agg}}^{\text{high}}) are pre-registered conventions.

Decision rule (H0).

· GO: \ge 2 targets passed within \tau_{\text{retro}}, aggregation difference < \Delta_{\text{agg}}^{\text{low}}, \kappa_{\text{func}} stability across \text{R\_strict}/\text{R\_moderate} below \theta_\kappa, unanchored-structural Sobol total index below \theta_{\text{sobol}}.
· CONDITIONAL GO: 1 target passed, or aggregation difference in [\Delta_{\text{agg}}^{\text{low}}, \Delta_{\text{agg}}^{\text{high}}], with excluded claims listed in the abstract.
· NO-GO: 0 targets passed, or aggregation difference > \Delta_{\text{agg}}^{\text{high}}, or unanchored-structural Sobol total index > \theta_{\text{sobol}}^{\text{hi}}.

All thresholds are conventions (§5.6). The NO-GO path is a publishable contribution: the thesis states that current public data cannot support closure claims and specifies the minimum data required.

5.3 Experimental design and disruption sampling

Four factors at three levels (inventory buffers, local sourcing, component redundancy, reconfiguration capability) give 3^4 = 81 configurations. Each configuration is crossed with sampled disruptions and multiple stochastic replications.

Disruptions are drawn from a Latin hypercube design over (f,d,k). The design partitions each dimension into n equiprobable strata and draws one point per stratum with independent random permutations, guaranteeing space-filling coverage and preventing correlation between dimensions. The 81 configurations are crossed with the disruption samples, yielding the multiverse execution matrix. The 81 configurations are not independent observations; inference uses hierarchical mixed-effects models with configuration random effects.

5.4 Anchoring standard and reflexive threshold ledger

Every parameter is labeled as anchored, unanchored-structural, or unanchored-operational. Unanchored-structural parameters are propagated through the multiverse as alternative graph and recipe-set specifications. Unanchored-operational parameters are propagated through Sobol global sensitivity analysis and reported as uncertainty bands.

Conclusion-stability criterion (primary). A hypothesis is supported only if its conclusion is stable across the interquartile range of the unanchored-operational ensemble and across the recipe-set variants. Report the fraction of the multiverse in which the conclusion holds, together with the stability curve versus percentile. The Sobol total index is reported as a descriptive diagnostic, not as a decision gate.

Reflexive application. The anchoring standard is applied to the thesis's own decision thresholds. \delta_{\min}, \tau_{\text{retro}}, \theta_\kappa, \theta_{\text{knee}}, \Delta_{\text{agg}}^{\text{low}}, \Delta_{\text{agg}}^{\text{high}}, \theta_{\text{sobol}}, the \Delta\text{AUC} margin, and \tau are entered into the provenance ledger as conventional unless an external estimate is cited for their specific value. A threshold labeled conventional is not weaker evidence than one labeled anchored—it is honest evidence—and the distinction is reported in the abstract and conclusions.

