Multiverse size. M_{\text{nominal}} is the product of axis cardinalities; M_{\text{effective}} is reported after defensibility pruning. For each cell m \in \{1,\dots,M_{\text{effective}}\}, estimate the H1 classification, the H2 \Delta\text{AUC}, and the H3 knee.

Inference layer: PIMA. Multiverse analysis is descriptive without an inferential procedure, and specification-curve inference is limited to simple linear models. PIMA provides a flexible and general inferential approach that considers all possible models and provides valid post-selection inference through a sign-flipping score test, with strong family-wise error control. Adjusted p-values and confidence intervals from PIMA are reported alongside the descriptive distribution of conclusions. The multiverse can be visualized as a specification curve sorted by \Delta V, exposing which axes flip aligned \leftrightarrow conflicting.

Conclusion stability. If the classification is identical across all M_{\text{effective}} cells, that stability is the inferential claim. If it flips, the modal conclusion and the fraction of cells supporting it are reported together, and the flipping axes are named explicitly.

