6. Computational architecture of the primitives

The framework is implemented as a modular, type-safe pipeline. The primitives below are specified at the level of data types and operators, permitting direct implementation and Lean 4 export.

6.1 Provenance and anchoring schema

The schema enforces the anchoring standard at data entry. Each record is immutable and carries a label from the set \{\text{anchored}, \text{unanchored-structural}, \text{unanchored-operational}, \text{conventional}\}. The provenance ledger aggregates records and is queried during 01C threshold calibration and during the reflexive audit of the thesis's own conventions.

6.2 Closure engine

The closure engine takes (M, E, K, R_{\text{variant}}) and returns (\mathcal{P}_R, \delta_R). The fixed-point computation iterates \Phi_R until no new node is added, accumulating energy expenditure and tracking the maximum input depth for each reproduced node. The three closure currencies and the borrowed-closure ratio are computed on the resulting set. The engine is deterministic given its inputs, which is a precondition for the confirmatory multiverse.

6.3 Pricing evaluator

The pricing evaluator computes V_{\text{net}} under P1–P5. P4a and P4b share identical constraints and differ only in the objective of the dual program; P5 introduces the pre-registered resilience weight \lambda. The evaluator does not choose a regime; it returns the vector of regime-specific values, and the multiverse treats regime as an axis.

6.4 Retrodiction gate runner

The gate runner holds two era-fixed targets. Each target carries an observed propagation ratio, an era policy with an inventory-buffer parameter, a disruption magnitude, and a duration. The runner computes a predicted ratio as a function of the graph vulnerability and the era buffer, compares it to the observed ratio within \tau_{\text{retro}}, and returns a decision in \{\text{GO}, \text{CONDITIONAL GO}, \text{NO-GO}\}. The era-fixed policy is enforced at target initialization, so a model cannot silently substitute a post-2011 Toyota buffer for the 2011 baseline.

6.5 Latin hypercube sampler

The sampler generates n stratified points in [0,1)^3 via independent random permutations, scales them to the physical bounds of (f,d,k), and crosses them with the 81 configurations. The resulting execution matrix is the input to the multiverse engine.

6.6 PIMA engine

The PIMA engine groups the execution matrix by multiverse cell, fits a generalized linear model within each cell, and applies a family-wise error correction across specifications. It returns, per cell, the coefficient, standard error, raw and adjusted p-values, and a preliminary conclusion class. At the multiverse level it returns the modal conclusion, the stability fraction, and the family-wise error level. The engine does not select a single specification; it reports the distribution of conclusions and the cells in which the conclusion is significant after correction.

