8. Expected contributions and value of null results

· Aligned (\Delta V > \delta_{\min} beyond f^*): efficient terrestrial design is a usable proxy beyond the divergence threshold.
· Independent (|\Delta V| \le \delta_{\min}): viability tells us nothing about resilience; resilience must be measured directly.
· Conflicting (\Delta V < -\delta_{\min}): a quantified cost of resilience (H3); terrestrial optimization does not transfer.
· NO-GO: public data cannot support the question; the structured minimum-data specification names exactly what is needed.
· Multiverse flips: the question is resolution- or recipe-conditional; the flipping axes are the actionable result.
· Knee unidentifiable: the Pareto relationship is too flat or too noisy to support a knee claim; reported as a scope limitation.

---

9. Limitations

· Results apply to the modeled system classes, not to space environments.
· The 81-point design is coarse; the multiverse partially compensates by treating design choices as axes.
· \kappa_{\text{info}} is a novel operationalization and inherits the uncertainty of its weighting scheme.
· The physical stage is a recipe adequacy probe, not full validation; if omitted, the CONDITIONAL GO is declared in the abstract.
· All numerical decision thresholds are conventional unless an external source is cited; the thesis reports this explicitly.
· Governance, build order, and physical-environment effects are out of scope.

