Appendix B — Formal properties of the closure operator

B.1 Definitions

Let V be a finite node set, R a recipe set, K the installed-equipment set, and E the energy budget. For X \subseteq V, define

\Phi_R^K(X) = X \;\cup\; \bigl\{ a \in V \;:\; \exists \rho \in R,\ \text{out}(\rho)=a,\ \text{in}(\rho) \subseteq X,\ \text{tools}(\rho) \subseteq K,\ \text{en}(\rho) \le E - \text{en}(X) \bigr\},

where \text{en}(X) = \sum_{\rho \in \mathcal{W}(X)} \text{en}(\rho) is the energy accumulated along the witnessing recipe set \mathcal{W}(X). Two modes are distinguished:

· Material mode. K and E are fixed exogenous inputs; \Phi_R^K depends only on X.
· Energy-augmented mode. Energy is accumulated along the iteration; the operator is applied with the monotone update \text{en}(X_{t+1}) = \text{en}(X_t) + \sum_{\rho \text{ newly fired}} \text{en}(\rho).

The material mode is the primary construction; the energy-augmented mode is reported as a sensitivity variant (App. B.4).

B.2 Monotonicity and fixed point

Proposition B.1 (Monotonicity). In material mode, X \subseteq Y \Rightarrow \Phi_R^K(X) \subseteq \Phi_R^K(Y).

Proof. Immediate: if \text{in}(\rho) \subseteq X and X \subseteq Y, then \text{in}(\rho) \subseteq Y, and all other conjuncts are independent of X. \square

Proposition B.2 (Least fixed point). (2^V, \subseteq) is a complete lattice with \bot = \emptyset and \top = V. By Proposition B.1 and the Knaster–Tarski theorem, \Phi_R^K has a least fixed point

\mathcal{P}_R^K = \operatorname{lfp}(\Phi_R^K) = \bigcup_{t \ge 0} \Phi_R^{K\,t}(\emptyset).

Proof. Knaster–Tarski for a monotone map on a complete lattice. The ascending Kleene chain converges by finiteness of V. \square

Proposition B.3 (Termination bound). The Kleene iteration terminates in at most |V| steps, because each non-trivial iteration adds at least one node and the lattice has |V|+1 distinct ranks.

Proposition B.4 (Complexity). Each iteration scans |R| recipes with set-membership checks costing O(\max_\rho |\text{in}(\rho)|). Total worst-case cost is

O\bigl(|V| \cdot |R| \cdot \bar{d}\bigr), \qquad \bar{d} = \max_{\rho \in R} |\text{in}(\rho) \cup \text{tools}(\rho)|.

For the intended scale (|V| \sim 10^{3}–10^{4}, |R| \sim 10^{3}, \bar{d} \sim 10), the material-mode computation is tractable in single-threaded Python; memoizing \text{in}(\rho) \subseteq X with bitsets yields a further factor of \approx 64 on typical hardware.

B.3 Chain depth

Along the witnessing iteration, set \delta_R(a) = 0 for a \in M, and for a node fired at step t by recipe \rho,

\delta_R(a) = 1 + \max_{u \in \text{in}(\rho)} \delta_R(u).

Because the witnessing recipe for a node is not unique when multiple recipes share an output, \delta_R is defined as the minimum over witnessing recipes (the shallowest available chain), and the maximum is reported as a robustness variant. Draft 0.4 reports the minimum as the primary and the maximum as a sensitivity, since shallow witnessing chains are the ones that survive adversarial recipe removal.

B.4 Energy-augmented mode

The energy-augmented operator is not monotone in general: a larger X may have consumed more energy, leaving less for a new recipe, so the fixed point may fail to exist under naive iteration. Two repairs are adopted:

1. Lexicographic mode. Order X by (energy consumed ascending, X ascending) and iterate to the lexicographic least fixed point. Existence follows from finiteness.
2. Budgeted-optimal mode. Solve the closure as a mixed-integer program: \max |\mathcal{P}| s.t. \text{en}(\mathcal{P}) \le E. This is NP-hard in general but tractable for |V| \le 10^{4} with a commercial MIP solver on a sparse recipe incidence matrix.

Material mode is reported as primary; lexicographic and budgeted-optimal are reported as sensitivity variants under the multiverse axis for closure semantics (App. L).

