Appendix E — Pricing regimes and shadow-price construction

E.1 Net viability

V_{\text{net}} = R - C_{\text{op}} - C_{\text{maint}} - C_{\text{repl}} - C_{\text{risk}}.

E.2 Regimes P1–P3

Regime Interpretation Multiplier \mu_P
P1 Terrestrial baseline prices 1
P2 High transport cost (mass tax) \mu_2, convention, calibrated in 01C
P3 Energy-constrained shadow multiplier \mu_3, convention, calibrated in 01C

V_{\text{net}}^{(P)} = R - \mu_P (C_{\text{op}} + C_{\text{maint}} + C_{\text{repl}} + C_{\text{risk}}). P2 and P3 multipliers are conventions; they are not literature-derived and are entered in the provenance ledger as such.

E.3 Regimes P4a and P4b — dual mass-constrained shadow prices

Let x \in \mathbb{R}^{n}_{+} be the activity vector over recipes, A \in \mathbb{R}^{m \times n}_{+} the resource-incidence matrix (rows: mass, energy, equipment-hours), b \in \mathbb{R}^{m}_{+} the resource endowment, and c \in \mathbb{R}^{n}_{+} the objective vector.

Primal (identical for P4a and P4b):

\max_{x \ge 0} \; c^{\top} x \quad \text{s.t.} \quad A x \le b.

Dual:

\min_{y \ge 0} \; b^{\top} y \quad \text{s.t.} \quad A^{\top} y \ge c.

The shadow price vector y is the dual optimum. P4a and P4b share the primal constraints and differ only in c:

· P4a — c^{\text{out}}_j = 1 for all j, maximizing aggregate output count.
· P4b — c^{\text{clo}}_j = \sum_{a \in \text{out}(\rho_j) \cap C_{\text{crit}}} w_{\text{closure}}(a), maximizing a linear surrogate for \kappa_{\text{func}}.

The w_{\text{closure}} weights are a pre-registered convention (set to uniform by default; sensitivity under unit weighting is reported). Sign changes of the V–resilience association between P4a and P4b are reported as objective-conditional.

E.4 Regime P5 — resilience-adjusted shadow prices

\max_{x \ge 0} \; \lambda \, \mathbf{1}^{\top} x + (1 - \lambda) \, \sum_{j} c^{\text{clo}}_j x_j \quad \text{s.t.} \quad A x \le b,

with \lambda \in [0,1] pre-registered. The dual y^{(5)}(\lambda) is reported as a function of \lambda on a fixed grid (e.g., \lambda \in \{0, 0.25, 0.5, 0.75, 1\}). P5 makes the normative trade-off between output and closure explicit rather than hiding it in the dual of a single-objective program.

E.5 Economic validity constraints

The shadow prices are used for comparison across configurations, not as market prices. Two guards apply: (i) the dual is computed only if the primal is feasible and bounded; infeasible configurations are reported as such and excluded from the V_{\text{net}} comparison; (ii) the shadow prices are compared across configurations within a regime, never across regimes, since the dual depends on the objective.

