Appendix G — PIMA specification

G.1 Problem

A multiverse with M_{\text{eff}} cells produces M_{\text{eff}} hypothesis tests. Selecting the cell that rejects the null most strongly inflates the family-wise error rate. A descriptive multiverse reports the distribution of conclusions but does not control error. Specification-curve inference controls error but is restricted to simple linear models. PIMA removes the linear restriction and provides valid post-selection inference.

G.2 Test statistic

Within cell m, let T_m be the test statistic for the null hypothesis of interest (e.g., \Delta V = 0 beyond f^*). Under the global null, T_m has a known distribution under sign-flipping of the residuals. The sign-flipping score test statistic is

T_m^{\text{score}} = \sum_{i=1}^{n_m} \varepsilon_{m,i} \, \mathbf{z}_{m,i}^{\top} \mathbf{w}_m,

where \varepsilon_{m,i} are the residuals under the null, \mathbf{z}_{m,i} the covariates, and \mathbf{w}_m a fixed weight vector. The null distribution of \max_m T_m^{\text{score}} is obtained by sampling B sign-flip permutations \epsilon^{(b)} \in \{-1,+1\}^{n_m} and recomputing T_m^{\text{score},(b)} for each m and b.

G.3 Family-wise error control

For a target FWER \alpha, the adjusted p-value for cell m is

p_m^{\text{adj}} = \frac{1 + \#\{b : \max_{m'} T_{m'}^{\text{score},(b)} \ge T_m^{\text{score}}\}}{1 + B}.

This is the standard max-statistic permutation procedure and controls FWER at \alpha in finite samples under exchangeability. Holm–Bonferroni is reported as an analytic alternative; the two are compared in 01C.

G.4 Practical notes

· B = 10^{4} permutations is the default; B = 10^{5} is used for the confirmatory run if runtime allows.
· Sign-flipping requires an approximately symmetric null distribution of residuals; this is checked by a symmetry diagnostic and, if it fails, a wild-bootstrap alternative is substituted.
· If M_{\text{eff}} is large (order 10^{2}–10^{3}), the max-statistic permutation procedure remains feasible because the cost is O(B M_{\text{eff}}), not O(B \cdot n_m) per cell.

G.5 Specification curve

The specification curve is a two-panel figure:

· Upper panel: \Delta V_m for each cell, ordered by magnitude, with 95% bootstrap bands.
· Lower panel: a matrix whose rows are the multiverse axes and whose columns are the ordered cells; each cell is marked with its axis setting.

The curve is descriptive; the inferential claim is carried by the PIMA-adjusted p-values. Reporting the two together prevents the curve from being read as a significance claim and prevents the PIMA output from being read without the structure of the underlying cells.

