H.4 Gate decision rule

Let N_{\text{pass}} be the number of targets whose predicted ratio falls inside the corresponding \tau_{\text{retro}}^{(i)}.

· GO: N_{\text{pass}} \ge 2, and the aggregation-bias difference < \Delta_{\text{agg}}^{\text{low}}, and the \kappa_{\text{func}} stability across R_{\text{strict}}/R_{\text{mod}} < \theta_\kappa, and the unanchored-structural Sobol total index < \theta_{\text{sobol}}.
· CONDITIONAL GO: N_{\text{pass}} = 1, or the aggregation-bias difference \in [\Delta_{\text{agg}}^{\text{low}}, \Delta_{\text{agg}}^{\text{high}}]. Excluded claims are listed in the abstract.
· NO-GO: N_{\text{pass}} = 0, or the aggregation difference > \Delta_{\text{agg}}^{\text{high}}, or the unanchored-structural Sobol total index > \theta_{\text{sobol}}^{\text{hi}}.

H.5 Aggregation-bias test

Let r^{\text{fine}} and r^{\text{agg}} be the predicted propagation ratios under the finest-available and the aggregated graph resolutions, respectively. Define

\Delta_{\text{agg}} = \frac{|r^{\text{fine}} - r^{\text{agg}}|}{\max(r^{\text{fine}}, r^{\text{agg}})}.

The establishment- and product-level evidence establishes the direction of the bias (aggregation understates propagation); the numerical thresholds \Delta_{\text{agg}}^{\text{low}}, \Delta_{\text{agg}}^{\text{high}} are conventions and are calibrated in 01C on the two retrodiction targets.

