Appendix K — Outcome measures: competing risks, RMTL, and censoring

K.1 Cumulative incidence

For M mutually exclusive failure modes, let

F_m(t) = \mathbb{P}(T \le t, \text{ failure mode } m), \qquad \sum_{m} F_m(t) \le 1.

The all-cause cumulative incidence is F_{\text{all}}(t) = \sum_m F_m(t).

K.2 RMTL

\text{RMTL}_m(\tau) = \int_0^\tau F_m(t)\, dt.

RMTL is interpretable as the expected number of time units lost to failure mode m up to horizon \tau. Under competing risks, it decomposes additively:

\sum_m \text{RMTL}_m(\tau) = \tau - \text{RMST}(\tau).

K.3 Transformations

Let p_m(\tau) = F_m(\tau) / \tau be the time-averaged incidence. The logit transform is

g_{\text{logit}}(\text{RMTL}_m) = \log\!\left(\frac{F_m(\tau)}{1 - F_m(\tau)}\right),

used when the event of interest is more frequent than competing events. The complementary log-log transform is

g_{\text{cloglog}}(\text{RMTL}_m) = \log\!\left(-\log(1 - F_m(\tau))\right),

used when competing events dominate. The choice is fixed per failure mode before 01D and is reported as a convention.

K.4 Censoring model

Let C_{c,d} \in \{0,1\} be the censoring indicator (1 if no failure within \tau). The logistic mixed model is

\operatorname{logit} \mathbb{P}(C_{c,d} = 1) = \alpha_0 + \boldsymbol{\alpha}_G \mathbf{g}_c + \alpha_I I_c + \alpha_R R_c + v_c,

with v_c \sim \mathcal{N}(0,\sigma_v^2). A large \boldsymbol{\alpha}_G indicates that censoring is associated with structural resilience, which biases the RMST comparison. The bias direction is reported: if censoring is more likely in structurally resilient configurations, the RMST comparison understates structural resilience relative to the RMTL comparison.

K.5 Function-level failure

For each f \in F_{\text{crit}} and threshold \theta_f, failure occurs at

T_f = \inf\{t \ge 0 : Q_f(t,S) < \theta_f\}.

The distribution of T_f across f is reported as a function-level survival curve. The horizon \tau is the same for all modes; the choice of \tau is a convention (App. I) and is sensitivity-tested over \{1.5, 2, 3\} \times median inventory depletion.

