Appendix O — Lean 4 export plan

The formalization targets the closure layer and the three currencies, not the pricing or PIMA layers, because the closure layer is the only layer whose statements are purely combinatorial and therefore directly formalizable.

O.1 Formalization scope

1. The node set V, the recipe relation, and the monotone operator \Phi_R are formalized as a Finset-based structure.
2. Monotonicity (Prop. B.1) is proved as a lemma.
3. The least fixed point is constructed via OrderHom.lfp on the complete lattice of Finset V.
4. The Kleene chain and the termination bound (Prop. B.3) are proved.
5. The three currencies are defined as rational-valued functions and their monotonicity in the reproduced set is proved: \kappa_{\text{func}}, \kappa_{\text{mass}}, \kappa_{\text{info}} are monotone non-decreasing in \mathcal{P}_R.

O.2 Export strategy

The Lean 4 module exports:

· closure : Finset V → Finset Tool → Finset V as an executable function, with a @[implemented_by] attribute linking to the Python implementation.
· Theorems Phi_monotone, lfp_exists, lfp_terminates, currencies_monotone.
· A decide-checkable predicate for a small finite test instance, used as a cross-check between the Lean executable and the Python implementation.

O.3 What is not formalized

The pricing layer (App. E) involves real-valued optimization and duals; it is not formalized. The retrodiction gate (App. H) involves empirical inputs and conventions; it is not formalized. The PIMA layer (App. G) involves permutation inference; it is not formalized. The formalization claim is therefore narrow: the closure operator and the three currencies are machine-checked; the rest of the pipeline is tested by the specification and by the small-instance cross-check.

